<h1>Provider <code>--help</code>, builtin <code>--help</code>, and a sturdier journal</h1>
<p>The model can read a provider's <code>--help</code> and a builtin's <code>--help</code> before it runs either, and the journal behind WhatsApp's conversation recall survives a restart without losing attachments.</p>
</header>
<p class="release-receipt"><strong>{{ release.tag }}</strong> · {{ release.date }} · <a href="{{ release.url }}">Release notes ↗</a> · <a href="{{ release.sourceUrl }}">{{ release.shortRevision }}</a>
</p>
<section id="changes"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-summary-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Five changes to providers, builtins, and the journal</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Change comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Change</th>
<th scope="col">Practical consequence</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Provider <code>--help</code> in the prompt</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">Every reachable provider command word's <code>--help</code> page renders once at broker load and rides the <code>bash</code> tool description as a new <code>command_word_help</code> field on the <code>Capabilities</code> frame — no per-session round trip, no liveness flash, capped around 2 KiB per word. Roll <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> and <code>dekopond</code> together; an old gateway refuses the frame outright.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Builtin <code>--help</code></th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">Any shell builtin answers <code>--help</code> when it's the script's literal, unexpanded only argument, naming the flags it accepts — the same accepted subset an <code>unsupported_flag</code> refusal already lists.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row"><code>efficiency</code> suggestions</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence"><code>suggest_improvement</code>'s <code>category</code> gains <code>efficiency</code>: a note about a workaround, a retry loop, or a multi-step dance one capability would have collapsed — the awkward path a session took even after succeeding, not just its failures.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">A journal that survives restarts</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence"><code>sessions.journal.maxBytes</code> is gone; a journal file now expires only after sitting unwritten longer than its longest route's <code>forgetAfterMs</code>. Lines carry the whole attachment inventory and drop the old 64 KiB text cap. This is WhatsApp's only recall path — it has no platform history to fall back on.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">The chart owns provider sync</th>
<td data-label="Practical consequence">Optional <code>broker.providerSync</code> hook Job runs <code>dekopon-brokerd provider sync</code> from a ConfigMap's <code>providers.yaml</code> into <code>broker.providerSet</code>, using the Deployment's own image. On Argo CD it needs a <code>Sync</code> wave, not Helm's pre-install/pre-upgrade hook.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="migration"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-details-title" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-path-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Six config keys break on upgrade.</h2>
<p>Delete these before the 0.26.0 daemons will start: <code>sessions.journal.maxBytes</code>, <code>chatMemory.maxDedupRecords</code>, <code>chatMemory.maxDedupBytes</code>, <code>improvementSuggestions</code>, and Slack's <code>liveness.stream: true</code> (only <code>false</code> is accepted now). Rename <code>storage.maxPendingTransactions</code> to <code>storage.maxActiveInvocations</code>; the value keeps its default of 64. Every one of these is refused as an unknown or invalid field, not silently ignored.</p>
<p>Order matters. Roll <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> and <code>dekopond</code> together for the new <code>command_word_help</code> frame. Upgrade <code>turso-sql</code> to 0.5.0 <strong>before</strong> the broker — the removed durable-files lock ladder refuses any component still importing it, taking the whole provider set down with it. Pin <code>memory-chat</code> to 0.5.0 in the same restart as the broker, or every <code>memory.chat.record</code> call fails until you do. <a href="{{ source }}/docs/upgrading.md">Full upgrade steps ↗</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/CHANGELOG.md">Full changelog ↗</a>
</p>
</section>
<section id="limits"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-scope-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>Redelivery isn't deduplicated anymore.</h2>
<p>WhatsApp's <code>ClaimedIds</code> ring and Discord's <code>SeenIds</code> ring are both gone. A WhatsApp retry that lands while the original turn is still running joins it as a steer; one that lands after the turn finished starts a fresh turn and answers twice — an accepted cost, not a bug. Discord's own gateway resume already caught that duplicate, so nothing changes there. Chat-memory's <code>record</code> no longer dedups either: a redelivered message is a second stored turn.</p>
<p>Slack stopped streaming the answer. Wait on live tokens over <code>slackSocketMode</code> and you'll get the progress message instead, or on Agent installations, <code>liveness.statusText</code>. Discord, Telegram, and the local transport still stream. And a pre-0.26 journal file isn't upgraded — the first recall after this release deletes it and starts empty, with nothing prior merged in.</p>
</section>
